TERM SHEET SUNDAY
Sunday, March 1, 2026

The week in venture, for founders who read the documents.

FEBRUARY IN NUMBERS
US venture deal count in February: 1,140, down 12% year on year. Dollars: $18.2B, up 9%, because four AI infrastructure rounds accounted for a third of it. Series A median pre-money: $42M, flat for three quarters. Seed median: $14M, down slightly. The market is bifurcated and has been for a year; the only new information is that the bifurcation is stable.

THE BRIDGE ROUND IS BACK, WITH BETTER MANNERS
Insider-led extensions were 22% of all early-stage rounds in February, the highest share since 2023. What is different from the last bridge wave: the terms are cleaner. Most are priced, not converting, and most come with a stated milestone for the next round rather than an open-ended runway extension. Existing investors have learned that an unpriced bridge with a discount creates a fight at the next round that nobody wins.

ONE CLAUSE TO READ TWICE
Information rights. The standard clause gives major investors monthly or quarterly financials. The clause to watch is the one that extends information rights to "any affiliate," which in a multi-strategy firm can mean the growth fund, the hedge fund and the public-markets desk. Ask for the affiliate language to be narrowed to the fund and its general partner. Most firms agree without argument; the ones that do not are telling you something.

ROUNDS THIS WEEK
- Corvid Robotics, $140M Series C, warehouse robotics, led by a growth fund.
- Halyard Cloud, pre-IPO secondary at a reported $9B.
- Three industrial-software Series As between $12M and $20M, all led by specialist funds, none by the generalists. Pattern worth noting.

See you next Sunday.

